From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Adrian Bularca <adrian.bularca@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 15:49:44 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] core: poll the single-instance lock wait

A thread waiting for the single-instance TA lock sleeps until the thread
that drops the lock sends it a wake-up notification through Linux. On
t234, StMM calls come from UEFI runtime services, and from there OP-TEE
cannot make RPCs to Linux. So when StMM drops the lock, the wake-up is
lost and the waiter (for example the fTPM driver probe) never wakes.

NVIDIA already works around this for mutexes with CFG_MUTEX_POLL_INTERVAL
("Enable mutex polling for StMM" in plat-tegra/conf.mk). Use the same
interval for the single-instance lock wait, so the waiter checks the lock
again after each interval. When CFG_MUTEX_POLL_INTERVAL is 0 or not set,
the code is unchanged.

Also print a wake-up that could not be sent at info level instead of
debug level. The line is evidence that a wake-up was lost and that only
polling can recover the waiter. Failed sleeps stay at debug level.

Upstream-Status: Pending
This should be sent to NVIDIA and to OP-TEE.

Signed-off-by: Adrian Bularca <adrian.bularca@gmail.com>
---
--- a/optee_os/core/kernel/tee_ta_manager.c
+++ b/optee_os/core/kernel/tee_ta_manager.c
@@ -76,9 +76,24 @@
 {
 	/* Requires tee_ta_mutex to be held */
 	if (tee_ta_single_instance_thread != thread_get_id()) {
-		/* Wait until the single-instance lock is available. */
+		/*
+		 * Wait until the single-instance lock is available.
+		 *
+		 * With CFG_MUTEX_POLL_INTERVAL set (t234), wake up at that
+		 * interval and check the lock again. StMM calls come from
+		 * UEFI runtime services, and from there OP-TEE cannot send
+		 * the wake-up notification to Linux. When StMM drops this
+		 * lock, the wake-up is lost, and a thread waiting here would
+		 * sleep forever. NVIDIA polls mutexes on t234 for the same
+		 * reason.
+		 */
 		while (tee_ta_single_instance_thread != THREAD_ID_INVALID)
+#if defined(CFG_MUTEX_POLL_INTERVAL) && CFG_MUTEX_POLL_INTERVAL
+			condvar_wait_timeout(&tee_ta_cv, &tee_ta_mutex,
+					     CFG_MUTEX_POLL_INTERVAL);
+#else
 			condvar_wait(&tee_ta_cv, &tee_ta_mutex);
+#endif
 
 		tee_ta_single_instance_thread = thread_get_id();
 		assert(tee_ta_single_instance_count == 0);
--- a/optee_os/core/kernel/wait_queue.c
+++ b/optee_os/core/kernel/wait_queue.c
@@ -38,7 +38,18 @@
 					 timeout_ms);
 	else
 		res = notif_send_sync(id + NOTIF_SYNC_VALUE_BASE);
-	if (res)
+	/*
+	 * A wake-up that cannot be sent is lost, and the waiter wakes only
+	 * if its wait polls (CFG_MUTEX_POLL_INTERVAL). This happens when StMM
+	 * (called from UEFI runtime services, where OP-TEE cannot reach Linux)
+	 * releases a lock that a thread waits for. Print it, as evidence of a
+	 * lost wake-up. Failed sleeps stay at debug level: StMM's sleeps
+	 * always fail, and printing them would flood the console.
+	 */
+	if (res && !wait)
+		IMSG("Wake-up for thread %d could not be sent (res %#"PRIx32")",
+		     id, res);
+	else if (res)
 		DMSG("%s thread %d res %#"PRIx32, cmd_str, id, res);
 
 	return res;
